# %% [markdown]
# ## 17. What you built, and what a real run adds
#
# Three 4-bit formats, written down to the bit: the nibble packing they share, an integer grid with a
# float scale, the E2M1 element, the E8M0 and E4M3 scales, the MXFP4 block and the two-level NVFP4 tensor,
# each equal to a numpy reference on every probe, and each used to round-trip the weights of a model
# that this notebook trained. Every number printed above was computed by the code that printed it.
#
# **This is a toy.** The model has a few tens of thousands of quantised weights in a single transformer block, a real
# language model has billions in dozens of layers, and weight error compounds across layers in a way one
# block cannot show. A real deployment also quantises activations and the key-value cache, may keep the
# largest-magnitude channels in higher precision, and runs packed kernels on hardware that decodes
# the scales itself. As of 13 November 2026 NVIDIA's Blackwell tensor cores implement NVFP4 and AMD's
# CDNA 4 matrix cores add the OCP MX formats, MXFP4 among them (`claims.yaml`). The same NVFP4 paper
# also describes training in the format, which needs techniques this lesson does not touch.
# What carries over is the method: a format is a choice of element grid, scale grid and block size, its
# cost is counted in stored bytes, and its quality is measured on the weights that matter.

